.Dd July 9, 2026
.Dt HELLO-MODEL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm hello-model
.Nd print five greetings from a model, English first
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the smallest example of the harness pattern: a deterministic
program wrapped around a single model inference.
It takes no arguments, feeds the instructions in
.Pa agent.org
to a model, and prints the model's response to standard output.
.Pp
The instructions ask for
.Dq hello world
in five languages, one per line, each formatted as
.Ql Language: greeting ,
with English first and four more languages chosen at random.
Changing the behavior means editing
.Pa agent.org ,
not the harness.
.Pp
.Nm
owns the output contract so the model cannot surprise the caller.
The response is captured from the model's standard output, an empty
response is an error, and a failed model invocation is an error.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width MODEL
.It Ev MODEL
The model command, defaulting to
.Ql claude .
It is left unquoted at the call site, so additional words split into
separate arguments, as in
.Ql claude --model x .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
It exits 1 when
.Pa agent.org
is missing, the model invocation fails, or the model returns an empty
response.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ hello-model
English: hello world
French: bonjour le monde
Dutch: hallo wereld
Norwegian: hei verden
Finnish: hei maailma
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sclr 1
